{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life story involves complex political maneuvers alongside military conflicts with surrounding Muslim powers while he navigated the treacherous waters of Crusader state politics. He became count after his father died and he spent much of his early reign consolidating power within his own territories before expanding his influence to the royal court. Raymond often acted as a regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and this position gave him immense authority over the kingdom despite facing opposition from rival noble factions.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited his title at a young age following the assassination of his father Raymond II by the Hashashin order. This tragic event forced him to assume leadership duties while still a minor and he relied on advisors to help manage the county defenses against external threats. His mother Hodierna played a significant role in his upbringing and she ensured he received proper education regarding governance and warfare tactics necessary for ruling a frontier state. The young count faced immediate challenges from Nur ad-Din who sought to exploit the instability caused by the leadership transition yet Raymond managed to maintain control over Tripoli through diplomatic alliances and strategic fortification efforts.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe period when Raymond served as regent for King Baldwin IV marked the height of his political career and influence within the kingdom. He advocated for peaceful coexistence with Saladin and opposed aggressive military campaigns that he believed would endanger the fragile stability of the Crusader states. This stance put him at odds with Guy of Lusignan and the Templar order who favored immediate confrontation with Muslim forces. Raymond argued that the kingdom lacked sufficient manpower to withstand a full-scale invasion and his warnings proved prophetic when the Battle of Hattin resulted in a catastrophic defeat for the Crusaders. His diplomatic approach alienated many hardline nobles but it demonstrated his pragmatic understanding of the geopolitical landscape.\n\n*Final Years and Legacy*\nRaymond retired from public life shortly after the disaster at Hattin and he died later in 1187 leaving behind a legacy of cautious diplomacy and strategic foresight. Historians often debate whether his advice could have prevented the loss of Jerusalem if the kingdom had followed his counsel more closely during the critical months before the battle. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the broader Crusader movement lost one of its most experienced leaders during a time of unprecedented crisis. The subsequent fall of Jerusalem validated his long-held fears about the dangers of reckless military adventurism in the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0001192164}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0016913404}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.1920936e-06}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0056690024}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.04692768}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.00027141676}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.6226078e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.9073505e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"t